const FAQS = [
 ['Is Agent Fabric the same as Agentforce?','No. Agentforce is an agent platform for building and operating agents. Agent Fabric is a control plane around an ecosystem of agents, MCP endpoints, and related services. Agentforce agents can be part of that ecosystem; supported bridging may be needed for A2A orchestration.','product'],
 ['Do I need a broker for every use case?','No. Start with the capability you need. For example, you can use discovery and governance without adding multi-agent orchestration. A broker becomes useful when a business request needs coordination across specialists or tools.','architecture'],
 ['Does this replace my existing agents or APIs?','Not by default. The aim is to discover and connect existing capabilities. MCP Bridge can expose selected API operations as tools, and an A2A bridge can help supported non-A2A agents participate. Compatibility and integration work still matter.','bridge'],
 ['What is the difference between A2A and MCP?','A2A supports agent-to-agent communication and task delegation. MCP exposes tools and context to AI clients. In this story, the broker delegates a job to a specialist through A2A; the specialist uses a tool through MCP. These are complementary protocols, not competing names for the same thing.','secure'],
 ['Is the broker just a language model?','No. A broker is a coordinated workflow with routing and execution behavior. It can use a language model for selected reasoning or generation steps, while deterministic graph transitions control other steps. The model is one dependency, not the whole architecture.','network'],
 ['Are policies enforced automatically everywhere?','No. Policies must be configured on the appropriate managed endpoints, and traffic must actually pass through those enforcement points. Registration alone is not protection. Shield markers here simplify the gateway routes; they do not imply that unproxied traffic is covered.','gateway'],
 ['What happens when a tool is not allowed?','Try the sensitive-data story. In our illustrative policy setup, a bulk-export tool is unavailable to the support identity. The attempted operation is denied before any customer records are returned. Production behavior depends on your access policies, protocol implementation, and error handling.','access'],
 ['Can I trace what happened and find bottlenecks?','Agent Visualizer helps you inspect network relationships, call volume, and governance gaps. Use the relevant monitoring and logging tools for deeper operational investigation. This website only displays authored events; it does not expose private model reasoning or live traces.','visualizer'],
 ['Where do agent networks run?','The current overview describes deployment to CloudHub 2.0 shared or private spaces, or a Runtime Fabric target. External specialists and systems can remain on their own platforms. Verify supported runtimes, topology, entitlements, and version-specific prerequisites for your deployment.','overview'],
 ['Does governance guarantee correct answers?','No. Access controls and model safeguards reduce particular risks; they do not make every generated answer correct. Ground responses in reliable tool results, evaluate behavior, handle failures, and design human approval for sensitive actions. This is an implementation responsibility, not a guarantee demonstrated by this page.','policies'],
 ['Is this an actual MuleSoft product screen?','No. It is an independent, customer-friendly explainer. Every request, outcome, policy check, and log entry is simulated. The animation is intentionally simplified; it is not a live Agent Visualizer, a benchmark, or a production deployment template.','overview']
];
